  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nLate evening KDDC radar observations show the decaying remnants of \nanother marginally severe thunderstorm complex are scattered over \nour western zones, with an outflow boundary racing eastward. This \nactivity will continue to taper off through the rest of the evening \nas the boundary layer stabilizes, and the majority of the overnight \nperiod will be quiet with lows dropping into the mid-60s northwest \nto the mid 70s southeast. Daytime Saturday, short range ensembles \nindicate an upper level ridge over the southeastern CONUS will \nremain nearly stationary, with southwesterly flow upstream over the \ncentral plains. Given the consecutive evenings with considerable \nrainfall that have reinforced a cool airmass over southwest KS, \nanother few degrees will be shaved off afternoon temperatures with \nhighs in the upper 80s/low 90s northwest to the low 100s southeast. \nHREF members all suggest thunderstorms are likely in our area for \nthe third consecutive evening, depicting convection initiating in \nsoutheast CO and growing upscale into a loosely organized cluster as \nit crosses into southwest KS. Models are showing a slightly higher \ndegree of organization as this complex moves through our western \nzones, and this is consistent with marginally stronger deep-layer \nwind shear of 25-30 kts. As a result, severe wind gusts may be \nmodestly more common Saturday evening, favoring areas west of US-83.\n\nDaytime Sunday, medium range ensembles agree the upper level ridge \nwill begin to retrograde, reaching the southern plains by 06Z \nMonday. Despite the ridge drawing nearer to the central plains, \npartly to mostly cloudy skies and recent precipitation will engender \na noticeably cooler afternoon as temperatures only peak in the low \nto mid 80s, save for the southeast zones who are expected to reach \nthe low 90s. Yet another round of late afternoon/early evening \nthunderstorms are possible, once again posing an isolated damaging \nwind gust risk.\n\nMonday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles suggest \nthe upper level ridge will remain flat over the southern CONUS \nthrough at least mid-business week, but begin to build northward \ninto the Intermountain West on Thursday and Friday. This synoptic \npattern evolution will keep afternoon temperatures in the 80s and \n90s through Friday, along with precipitation chances favoring Monday \nand Wednesday afternoon/evening.",
